WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.918
- You had to come with
a lil' bit more

00:00:01.918 --> 00:00:03.837
than just the best players.

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:07.298
All them people that they say
are the best to ever do it—

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:09.342
- Yeah?
- My teams beat all them.

00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:22.105
- Welcome to Full-Bodied.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:23.231
I'm Channing Frye.

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:26.151
As the wines get darker, the
questions gonna get deeper.

00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:27.193
Let's go.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.697
Isiah Thomas. Zeke.
Coach. Boss. Mentor.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:34.242
First of all, thank you so much
for taking the time to be here

00:00:34.242 --> 00:00:35.577
with us on Full-Bodied.

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:39.247
It's amazing to me to be able
to open up another African

00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:41.541
American man's Champagne.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:42.709
- Oh, most definitely.

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:44.377
This is one of my
favorite things

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:47.672
about being in the Champagne
and the wine spaces,

00:00:47.672 --> 00:00:53.053
that you get to, you know,
pop and drink and sip.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:56.473
And you get to have
great conversation.

00:00:56.473 --> 00:01:00.518
- Where did this idea or
opportunity come from?

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:04.064
You're an inner-city
kid from Chicago, and you're—

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:06.483
are you the only
Black man that owns

00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:09.527
Champagne grapes in Champagne?

00:01:09.527 --> 00:01:12.489
- I'm the only Black man, and
I'm the only American that

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:14.783
owns the grapes in Champagne.

00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:17.660
As you know, they won't let
an American own land there.
- Right.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.455
- So we own all the
rights to the grapes

00:01:20.455 --> 00:01:23.083
that come off the land.
That allows us to give you

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.501
the first press of the grape.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.253
And what I discovered is
that, here in America,

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:30.882
a lot of the Champagnes that we
get are second and third press.

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:33.802
Loaded with sugar.
Very high in sulfites.

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:36.054
So consequently, you get a
headache when you drink it.

00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:39.974
This is our Le Champion,
and it is 100 percent pinot.

00:01:39.974 --> 00:01:43.853
You probably won't find another
100 percent pinot in the United States.

00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:45.939
And if you do, it's
maybe one or two of 'em.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:46.773
- This is delicious.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.400
You've had every single
job. You've been a coach.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.445
You've been a GM. You've been
an owner. You've been a player.

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:56.241
When you were playing, did
you know, "When I get done,

00:01:56.241 --> 00:01:58.326
these are the things that
I want to accomplish"?

00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:00.870
- Even when I was playing,
I was what you might

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.831
call a serial entrepreneur.

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:05.708
I experimented a
lot, you know, in business.

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:08.711
And a lot of it came
around the city of Detroit

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:09.712
and in Michigan.

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:13.466
I started a candy bar called
Isiah Bar after the Reggie

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:16.094
Jackson bars, after
the Reggie! bar.

00:02:16.094 --> 00:02:19.264
Put that on the shelf, and then
I partnered with Stroh's ice

00:02:19.264 --> 00:02:19.764
cream.

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:23.309
Put Stroh's ice cream into the
supermarkets, into the shelf.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.687
And then the next product,
as you talked about,

00:02:25.687 --> 00:02:27.939
was popcorn, because
I love popcorn.

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:30.650
- Yep. Oh, I know it.
- So we—

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.068
- Get you outta that Times Square.

00:02:32.068 --> 00:02:33.153
That popcorn was hittin'.
- Yeah.

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.654
- I'm a popcorn guy, man.

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:35.655
- Yeah, you remember that.

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:36.865
- I see why you
got a Champagne.

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:38.324
You're celebrating
all these wins.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:41.202
You ain't got no losses.

00:02:41.202 --> 00:02:44.581
- Well, this Champagne
is called Le Champion.

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:46.207
But you gotta look at
this color, Channing,

00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:51.754
I'm always fascinated by how, you know,
the color of the Champagne is

00:02:51.754 --> 00:02:54.007
and how tight the bubbles are.

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:57.385
And, Channing, you'll notice,
as this Champagne kinda settles

00:02:57.385 --> 00:02:59.512
a little bit, it
gets a lot smoother.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.472
And that's what I love
about this Champagne.

00:03:01.472 --> 00:03:03.641
Some Champagnes you drink,
and you have a whole fight

00:03:03.641 --> 00:03:06.477
in your mouth, where you—
- [Laughing]

00:03:06.477 --> 00:03:09.147
- You know, you have to swish
it around and stuff—
- For sure.

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:11.608
- Before you can even swallow it.

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:13.276
- So, the Erath.

00:03:13.276 --> 00:03:18.448
- When I first saw this, you know, I'm old and my eyes ain't so good, right?

00:03:18.448 --> 00:03:19.866
But I play it off
cuz I don't

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:21.576
wanna put the glasses on, so.
- Right. Oh, yeah.

00:03:21.576 --> 00:03:24.245
- So when I first looked
at it, I was like,

00:03:24.245 --> 00:03:26.080
"Oh, this Earth Champagne."

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.707
I mean, "Earth wine."

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.959
I called it "Earth."

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:32.086
- Yeah, this is Earth,
Wind and Fire's wine.

00:03:32.086 --> 00:03:33.546
- Yeah, yeah. Exactly, exactly.

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:35.465
- It only comes out in September.

00:03:35.465 --> 00:03:36.132
- Ah, there you go.

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:38.176
That's nice. I like that.

00:03:38.176 --> 00:03:39.969
- This is a white pinot noir.

00:03:39.969 --> 00:03:42.847
Erath has been around and is
one of the better producers

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:45.767
in the Willamette Valley.
Whether that's smoke

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.560
or whether that's
something going

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.854
on with the skins of the grape—
- Mm.

00:03:49.854 --> 00:03:52.315
- They have to take the
skins off and press, right?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:56.361
And so you're getting the
essence of the pinot noir

00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:58.154
without the color.
- Yeah.

00:03:58.154 --> 00:04:02.116
- Let's go back just a little bit
farther. You told me a story.

00:04:02.116 --> 00:04:05.745
You growing up, having
your coach in your house.

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:08.373
You're on your recruiting visit
with your mom and your brother

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:09.249
there.
- Yeah.

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:12.710
- Tell everybody that story, and
then I'll bring it back around

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:13.461
to this wine.

00:04:13.461 --> 00:04:16.339
- So I'm assuming that you're
talking about Coach

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:18.967
Knight when I was
being recruited

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:20.802
to go to Indiana University.

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:25.390
And as you know, we were poorer than poor.
When Coach Knight came to visit,

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.267
we had no electricity.

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:31.187
And so when he came over,
all my brothers was there.

00:04:31.187 --> 00:04:34.649
You know, my mom was there. And we had
been offered a lot of things

00:04:34.649 --> 00:04:38.236
to go to different colleges. You know, money—
- Right.

00:04:38.236 --> 00:04:40.154
- And, you know, cars and homes.

00:04:40.154 --> 00:04:41.781
And again, we had nothing.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:42.490
- Whew, blue chips! Blue chips!

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:46.536
- Yeah, I mean, Channing, I actually
turned down blue chips.

00:04:46.536 --> 00:04:47.578
- It came back to you.
It came back.

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:50.540
- Yeah, so we got offered
all kinds of things, right?

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.167
And this one college
coach came to our house,

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:57.046
opened up a briefcase and said,
"Hey, there's $50,000 in here.

00:04:57.046 --> 00:05:00.466
If your son comes to this
college, you get $50,000."

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:03.720
Now, Channing, we had never seen
$50,000 before in our life.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.763
- Whew!
- As far as we know, it could have only

00:05:05.763 --> 00:05:10.310
been $1,000 in that briefcase.
- Right.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:11.978
- And so now, Coach Knight comes.

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:13.855
And I remember him
sitting there saying, you know,

00:05:13.855 --> 00:05:16.899
"Mrs. Thomas, you know I'm only gonna
offer your son three things.

00:05:16.899 --> 00:05:18.776
A. He's gonna get
a good education.

00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:20.570
B. I'm gonna teach
him everything I know

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.113
about the game of basketball.

00:05:22.113 --> 00:05:24.365
And C. He'll be a gentleman."

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:27.869
You know, I remember my brothers looking
at me and me looking at them, like,

00:05:27.869 --> 00:05:28.453
"Well, OK.

00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:29.996
Well, we're not
going to Indiana.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:32.415
I mean, this is—"
- [Laughing]

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:34.208
- "This ain't gonna work,"
you know?

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:37.587
And so, Channing, so now
the lights are going down.

00:05:37.587 --> 00:05:39.547
So it's getting
dark in the house.

00:05:39.547 --> 00:05:43.343
And as it's getting a little
darker, you know, we had roaches, right?

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:46.679
- Yeah.
- So there's a roach that's crawling right

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:48.473
behind Coach Knight.

00:05:48.473 --> 00:05:51.517
And I'm like, "Oh, please
go back. Please go back."

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:52.852
- [Laughing]

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.188
- And the roach is
crawling down the wall.

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:57.023
And I'm saying to
myself, "Oh, man, I

00:05:57.023 --> 00:06:00.568
hope don't nobody see that
roach coming down the wall."
- [Laughing]

00:06:00.568 --> 00:06:03.112
- So now, you know, the conversation gets,

00:06:03.112 --> 00:06:05.573
you know, they get to talking about
where are you going to play.

00:06:05.573 --> 00:06:09.035
And, you know, how's it going
to be at Indiana.

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:12.789
My mom asked Coach Knight, you know,
a pretty serious question.

00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:17.210
She said, you know, the klan is
in Martinsville, Indiana.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:21.047
If my son gets into
any type of issues,

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:23.132
who's going to protect him?

00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:28.429
He said, well, if we're
winning, Mrs. Thomas, the klan

00:06:28.429 --> 00:06:31.140
will protect him.
[Both laughing]

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:32.183
- Oh, my God.

00:06:32.183 --> 00:06:33.810
He said that to your mother?

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.020
- Yeah, but, you know, in a joking kind of
way, right?

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:39.774
My brother's like you. Like,
he didn't think that was a joke.
- [Laughing]

00:06:39.774 --> 00:06:43.861
- So he was like, yo, what the
- Yeah, yeah.
- F you mean, so forth and so on.

00:06:43.861 --> 00:06:46.864
Coach Knight was like, well, you know,
it's just a joke and dah, dah…

00:06:46.864 --> 00:06:49.075
And so now I look over at
my brother, and he's lit.

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:50.451
Now he ready to fight.

00:06:50.451 --> 00:06:53.079
And Coach Knight says
something back to him.

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:56.791
And then he said, well, you know, if
you wanna do something now,

00:06:56.791 --> 00:06:59.085
we can take this outside.

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:03.714
And I never forget, Coach Knight
got up, took his jacket off,

00:07:03.714 --> 00:07:05.425
rolled up his sleeves
- [Laughing]

00:07:05.425 --> 00:07:08.761
- and said, yeah, we
can go outside.

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:11.806
And so now the whole
house is in an uproar, right?

00:07:11.806 --> 00:07:15.184
My mother is sitting there,
And, you know, I'm looking at her

00:07:15.184 --> 00:07:16.769
for her reaction, right?

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:18.229
- For guidance, like,

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:20.273
what am I supposed
to do with this?

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:22.275
- Yeah, and she's sitting
there like this…

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:28.156
 And I'm like,
- [Laughing]
- oh, no.

00:07:28.156 --> 00:07:31.367
She like him. Right?

00:07:31.367 --> 00:07:33.536
And so it didn't
get into nothing.

00:07:33.536 --> 00:07:35.288
You know, they broke it up and everything.

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:37.957
And my mom had a
press conference.

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:39.542
I was sitting next to her.

00:07:39.542 --> 00:07:42.670
And she said, my son
has made his decision.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:46.090
He's decided that he's
going to Indiana University

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:48.134
to play for Coach Bob Knight.

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:52.138
And I was sitting
there like this.

00:07:52.138 --> 00:07:53.389
But it turned out to be one of the best
decisions

00:07:53.389 --> 00:07:54.557
- You had no choice.

00:07:54.557 --> 00:07:55.558
- she made.
- [Laughing] Yeah.

00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:58.186
Here's what I'll say about that.

00:07:58.186 --> 00:08:01.272
Both of us have been
coached by amazing coaches
- Mhm.

00:08:01.272 --> 00:08:04.817
- throughout our NBA career,
throughout our college career.

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:07.862
And one thing they
didn't do was look at us

00:08:07.862 --> 00:08:09.530
based on our skin.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:13.159
They looked at us as
men. They looked at us

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:15.953
as how can they, how
could I make them better.
- Yes.

00:08:15.953 --> 00:08:17.330
- Like, how can I,

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:21.292
how can they make the other
pieces that I have better, right?

00:08:21.292 --> 00:08:23.211
And I feel like, when
you have an opportunity

00:08:23.211 --> 00:08:27.673
to have a vineyard, right, and
things don't go the exact way.

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:30.927
You're looking at: How can
I still tell this story?
- Right.

00:08:30.927 --> 00:08:32.762
- And so for me, this
white pinot noir

00:08:32.762 --> 00:08:36.891
forever will be, whether it is
2015 or whether it's

00:08:36.891 --> 00:08:38.267
2020

00:08:38.267 --> 00:08:40.770
or whatever year,
white pinot to me

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:45.525
is making the best out of
sometimes tough situations.

00:08:45.525 --> 00:08:49.946
I think you're different, right, from a
lot of GMs or a lot of owners

00:08:49.946 --> 00:08:50.863
and a lot of coaches.

00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:54.617
Because they want to shy away
from the personal relationships

00:08:54.617 --> 00:08:57.328
with players, right? That
is not business.

00:08:57.328 --> 00:09:00.915
That relationship and that
trust is, some people just,

00:09:00.915 --> 00:09:05.419
they put players on a carousel,
- Yeah.
- right? Without getting to know them.

00:09:05.419 --> 00:09:07.296
And they wanna
talk—you can't talk

00:09:07.296 --> 00:09:08.798
to each player the same way.

00:09:08.798 --> 00:09:10.550
To tell you something
even crazier is

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:11.842
I still live in Portland.

00:09:11.842 --> 00:09:15.179
You sent me where I've
lived for 14 years.
- Yeah.

00:09:15.179 --> 00:09:18.432
- We're gonna open
up this last pinot.

00:09:18.432 --> 00:09:21.352
When you were playing, who were guys

00:09:21.352 --> 00:09:25.398
that you felt like you had to have the utmost preparedness

00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:27.275
for and respect?

00:09:27.275 --> 00:09:29.694
Obviously, it was Jordan.
Obviously, it was Bird.

00:09:29.694 --> 00:09:31.112
Obviously, it was Magic.

00:09:31.112 --> 00:09:33.614
But who were some of the
guys, some of the unknowns

00:09:33.614 --> 00:09:37.159
that you were like, this
dude can go but just

00:09:37.159 --> 00:09:38.703
doesn't get enough credit for it?

00:09:38.703 --> 00:09:40.496
- The first name that
comes to mind right now

00:09:40.496 --> 00:09:43.833
is Maurice Cheeks, who just
went into the Hall of Fame.

00:09:43.833 --> 00:09:46.168
Now, Mo Cheeks is
from Chicago also.

00:09:46.168 --> 00:09:49.964
And I remember when I
first came into the league, you know,

00:09:49.964 --> 00:09:51.716
Philadelphia was rolling.

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:56.262
Channing, they was rolling.
I mean, he, Andrew Toney, you know,

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:59.265
Bobby Jones, Dr.
J, Moses Malone.

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:01.892
It was like, Lionel
Hollins, he was like, damn.

00:10:01.892 --> 00:10:04.020
Like, where do we get a break?

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:10.318
You know, most guards would slide their
feet, but Mo Cheeks hopped—
- Yeah.

00:10:10.318 --> 00:10:12.028
- You know. And so that gave him—
- [Laughing]

00:10:12.028 --> 00:10:15.948
- That gave him an extra
quickness and an extra bounce.

00:10:15.948 --> 00:10:21.787
And so I would go through my stuff,
nuh, nuh, nuh, nuh, nuh, nuh…nuh,
nuh, nuh…real hard between my legs.

00:10:21.787 --> 00:10:26.250
Give my best shake,
and he'll say, huh.
[Laughing]

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:27.501
And I was like, oh, man.

00:10:27.501 --> 00:10:28.794
- He'd just be, still be there.

00:10:28.794 --> 00:10:30.338
- Still be there.
- Oh.

00:10:30.338 --> 00:10:31.756
- And I couldn't shake him.
- Yeah, he's like…

00:10:31.756 --> 00:10:35.426
- But he was the guy that gave me
the most problems defensively.

00:10:35.426 --> 00:10:37.637
- These are stories
that I think we

00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:40.890
need to continue to tell
about different players.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:45.811
You know, in today's media, we always talk
about the top 20 or the top 50.

00:10:45.811 --> 00:10:49.440
But each generation, there
are so many players that get

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:52.526
overlooked that had
a huge influence.

00:10:52.526 --> 00:10:55.112
Like, there's no James Harden
if Manu Ginobili doesn't

00:10:55.112 --> 00:10:56.405
come with that Eurostep first.

00:10:56.405 --> 00:10:57.114
- Yes.

00:10:57.114 --> 00:10:57.365
Yes.

00:10:57.365 --> 00:10:57.865
- Right?

00:10:57.865 --> 00:10:59.325
And that should be the story.

00:10:59.325 --> 00:11:03.412
Like, James Harden is amazing, but
Manu Ginobili, like—and then here's

00:11:03.412 --> 00:11:03.954
the truth.

00:11:03.954 --> 00:11:06.415
We swept the Spurs in 2010.

00:11:06.415 --> 00:11:09.210
You know who was the only dude
that we had to double-team?

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:12.254
It was Manu Ginobili when they
ran that
- Wow.
- sideline screen,

00:11:12.254 --> 00:11:14.006
when they ran that 1-4, 1-5.

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:17.468
If you had to have, like, a
starting five of these dudes

00:11:17.468 --> 00:11:19.970
are just hoopers who
don't get enough credit

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:23.265
from your generation, who would
they be, starting with guards?

00:11:23.265 --> 00:11:26.519
- The coaching of today is
so drastically different

00:11:26.519 --> 00:11:27.561
than it was back then.

00:11:27.561 --> 00:11:31.732
I'm sure Magic Johnson would
have probably had two or three

00:11:31.732 --> 00:11:35.945
coaches fired because the
coaches would have been looking

00:11:35.945 --> 00:11:39.532
at him, like, oh, well,
you a 6'9" center

00:11:39.532 --> 00:11:42.410
and you don't shoot the
three, so we can't play you

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:44.829
at the point guard position.

00:11:44.829 --> 00:11:46.747
[Laughing]

00:11:46.747 --> 00:11:47.998
You know?

00:11:47.998 --> 00:11:49.709
- I mean, you're not wrong. I think…

00:11:49.709 --> 00:11:52.169
- And so in this
era of basketball,

00:11:52.169 --> 00:11:54.588
it would've been
interesting to see, you know,

00:11:54.588 --> 00:11:58.426
who would've been successful
from our era in this era

00:11:58.426 --> 00:12:01.345
and what positions would've
had to change

00:12:01.345 --> 00:12:04.140
and who would have
the nerve as a coach

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:08.978
to change their coaching style
to make those guys successful.

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:12.565
- I think a lot of coaches coach
what makes them successful, not

00:12:12.565 --> 00:12:15.443
coaching to win and what's
best for their players,

00:12:15.443 --> 00:12:17.403
even though it's
uncomfortable for them.

00:12:17.403 --> 00:12:17.903
- Yeah.

00:12:17.903 --> 00:12:18.404
- Right?

00:12:18.404 --> 00:12:20.906
And I think some coaches
go: "This is what I know.

00:12:20.906 --> 00:12:22.283
This is what we're going to do."

00:12:22.283 --> 00:12:24.744
Culturally, you can
do that as a coach.

00:12:24.744 --> 00:12:27.079
But when it comes to the
skills and the talents

00:12:27.079 --> 00:12:29.749
of your players, your
offense and defense

00:12:29.749 --> 00:12:33.919
has to be 100 percent representative
of who's out there playing.

00:12:33.919 --> 00:12:34.420
- Yeah.

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:35.337
- You're coaching.

00:12:35.337 --> 00:12:36.756
You're not playing.

00:12:36.756 --> 00:12:40.217
- How many players really
have in their vocabulary,

00:12:40.217 --> 00:12:44.722
what you just articulated
is I'm playing to make

00:12:44.722 --> 00:12:47.767
winning plays to win the game?

00:12:47.767 --> 00:12:48.267
- [Laughing] Yeah.

00:12:48.267 --> 00:12:51.979
- I'm not trying to cover
my ass for stats, right?

00:12:51.979 --> 00:12:54.648
- I was the beginning of
the social media era.

00:12:54.648 --> 00:12:55.149
- Right.

00:12:55.149 --> 00:12:57.735
- What happened was, Zeke,
and you can attest to this.

00:12:57.735 --> 00:13:00.905
When you would lose, in the
newspaper, they would say,

00:13:00.905 --> 00:13:03.616
so-and-so lost, and
then that was it.

00:13:03.616 --> 00:13:06.118
There wasn't no highlight
tape where you could cut it,

00:13:06.118 --> 00:13:06.994
where you're dunking.

00:13:06.994 --> 00:13:07.286
- Nope.

00:13:07.286 --> 00:13:09.705
- You could be down 50, and people
are like, man, he's so good.

00:13:09.705 --> 00:13:10.372
No, you're not good.

00:13:10.372 --> 00:13:11.207
You're not winning.

00:13:11.207 --> 00:13:11.791
- Yes.

00:13:11.791 --> 00:13:12.082
- Right?

00:13:12.082 --> 00:13:12.583
- Yeah.

00:13:12.583 --> 00:13:13.793
- And so for me—

00:13:13.793 --> 00:13:14.752
- You lost.

00:13:14.752 --> 00:13:17.254
- Players are falling in love
with the fact—you lost.

00:13:17.254 --> 00:13:17.755
- Yeah.

00:13:17.755 --> 00:13:20.007
- You're a loser, right?

00:13:20.007 --> 00:13:20.508
- Yeah.

00:13:20.508 --> 00:13:22.176
- That hurt, right?

00:13:22.176 --> 00:13:23.928
If you weren't at the
championship game,

00:13:23.928 --> 00:13:25.721
you weren't playing
at the Phoenix Suns' arena,

00:13:25.721 --> 00:13:27.473
or America West Arena,

00:13:27.473 --> 00:13:29.266
you didn't have all
the girls on you,

00:13:29.266 --> 00:13:29.767
- Yeah.

00:13:29.767 --> 00:13:30.434
- people were like, ugh.

00:13:30.434 --> 00:13:30.935
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:13:30.935 --> 00:13:32.144
- What are you, like, fourth?

00:13:32.144 --> 00:13:32.645
Gross.

00:13:32.645 --> 00:13:33.145
- Yeah.

00:13:33.145 --> 00:13:33.771
- Right?

00:13:33.771 --> 00:13:34.814
It hurt.

00:13:34.814 --> 00:13:36.732
Hey, my dad used to
drop me off at six

00:13:36.732 --> 00:13:38.567
in the morning at
this place called

00:13:38.567 --> 00:13:40.736
Kiwanis Park in
Chandler, Arizona.

00:13:40.736 --> 00:13:42.279
Cuz six in the
morning, he would

00:13:42.279 --> 00:13:43.614
have me play against grown men.

00:13:43.614 --> 00:13:44.114
- Yes.

00:13:44.114 --> 00:13:44.615
- Right?

00:13:44.615 --> 00:13:46.158
So now I'm 12, 13, 14.

00:13:46.158 --> 00:13:47.493
I'm playing 30-year-old men.

00:13:47.493 --> 00:13:47.993
- Yeah.

00:13:47.993 --> 00:13:48.536
- I'm 6-foot-2.

00:13:48.536 --> 00:13:50.454
They think I'm a little
bigger than what I am.

00:13:50.454 --> 00:13:52.581
- That's where you learn
shot selection at.
- Yeah.

00:13:52.581 --> 00:13:55.000
[Both laughing]

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.002
Well, that's where
you learn—listen,

00:13:57.002 --> 00:14:00.089
you don't want to be somewhere
from 6 a.m. to 2 p.m.

00:14:00.089 --> 00:14:01.966
and be sitting on the
side every single time.

00:14:01.966 --> 00:14:02.508
- Yeah.

00:14:02.508 --> 00:14:04.760
- To switch back to
this wine, right?

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:07.096
So this pinot noir is Granville.

00:14:07.096 --> 00:14:08.931
It's the farmhouse cuvée.

00:14:08.931 --> 00:14:10.349
Jackson is the man.

00:14:10.349 --> 00:14:11.475
- Yeah, this is nice.

00:14:11.475 --> 00:14:13.727
- Jackson is a second generation.

00:14:13.727 --> 00:14:15.855
His dad was a grower.

00:14:15.855 --> 00:14:19.483
He knows the Willamette Valley
like the back of his hand.

00:14:19.483 --> 00:14:21.610
He's making it more new age.

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:25.030
This is a wine that is, like, the
middle-of-the-week wine, where

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:27.825
you're like, I just
wanna pure pinot noir.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:27.825 --> 00:14:30.202
- I want what Willamette
Valley tastes like.

00:14:30.202 --> 00:14:31.036
And this is it.

00:14:31.036 --> 00:14:34.582
- It's a smooth, you know, goes down easy.

00:14:34.582 --> 00:14:36.083
- There is no new oak on this.

00:14:36.083 --> 00:14:40.337
And that, to me, is what
makes really good wine.

00:14:40.337 --> 00:14:41.088
It's restraint.

00:14:41.088 --> 00:14:43.299
And, for me, it's drinkable.

00:14:43.299 --> 00:14:45.050
It's approachable.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:46.802
It's, like, not, you don't feel a
certain way about it

00:14:46.802 --> 00:14:48.178
except this is delicious.

00:14:48.178 --> 00:14:52.766
- I remember one of my
first tastings, right?

00:14:52.766 --> 00:14:54.977
And they said, you know,
spit it out, right?

00:14:54.977 --> 00:14:55.978
And I was like, OK.

00:14:55.978 --> 00:14:57.146
And I did that.
- [Laughing]

00:14:57.146 --> 00:15:01.609
- As the day went on,you know,  you just
started smelling it, right?

00:15:01.609 --> 00:15:02.109
- Oh, yeah.

00:15:02.109 --> 00:15:04.194
- And what I didn't know
is that you can

00:15:04.194 --> 00:15:07.156
get a high from smelling it, like.

00:15:07.156 --> 00:15:07.907
- Oh, man.

00:15:07.907 --> 00:15:09.700
Is that what got you
in the other industry?

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:11.869
- If you just walk around and
you're just smelling it,

00:15:11.869 --> 00:15:12.578
you're like…

00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:14.747
Actually, you know what got
me in the other industry?

00:15:14.747 --> 00:15:16.790
In the cannabis
and the hemp space,

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.167
it's actually my son.
- Oh.

00:15:18.167 --> 00:15:21.879
- My son and my daughter, you know,
they're younger, so.

00:15:21.879 --> 00:15:23.005
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- You know,

00:15:23.005 --> 00:15:23.923
- You would assume.

00:15:23.923 --> 00:15:25.424
- they indulge, right?

00:15:25.424 --> 00:15:29.345
[Laughing] But then my son and
my daughter started saying,

00:15:29.345 --> 00:15:33.057
hey, you know, you need to start
studying this a little bit more

00:15:33.057 --> 00:15:35.935
because it helps
with my anxiety.

00:15:35.935 --> 00:15:37.269
It helps with my depression.

00:15:37.269 --> 00:15:37.853
- Right.

00:15:37.853 --> 00:15:39.521
- My daughter has narcolepsy.

00:15:39.521 --> 00:15:41.523
And, you know, I wore shoes today.

00:15:41.523 --> 00:15:42.858
- Yeah, yeah. So show us those shoes.

00:15:42.858 --> 00:15:44.026
- The Sleep Project.

00:15:44.026 --> 00:15:45.653
Everybody should check it out.

00:15:45.653 --> 00:15:48.280
But my daughter was
diagnosed with narcolepsy.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:50.699
The doctors were saying,
hey, why don't you

00:15:50.699 --> 00:15:53.786
try a little, you know, marijuana
for your depression

00:15:53.786 --> 00:15:55.454
or your anxiety
- Right, right.
- or whatever.

00:15:55.454 --> 00:15:57.623
That's when I really started
looking into the plant.

00:15:57.623 --> 00:15:59.041
But I've gotten
into the business.

00:15:59.041 --> 00:16:00.834
I'm vice chairman,
CEO of this company

00:16:00.834 --> 00:16:02.336
called One World Pharma.

00:16:02.336 --> 00:16:05.547
- If you had to get five
players from today's game

00:16:05.547 --> 00:16:11.929
and play a seven-game series
against your bad boy Pistons,

00:16:11.929 --> 00:16:13.263
who would those players be?

00:16:13.263 --> 00:16:15.683
- The question is, what
rules are we playing by?

00:16:15.683 --> 00:16:17.601
- We're playing by the '80s rules.

00:16:17.601 --> 00:16:18.394
Your rules.

00:16:18.394 --> 00:16:21.188
- Imma pick a team that
talent-wise would give us

00:16:21.188 --> 00:16:23.190
a hard time.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:24.233
- You would break them.

00:16:24.233 --> 00:16:29.363
- Yeah, well, our mentality was…
- [Laughing]
- and what our defensive scheming

00:16:29.363 --> 00:16:32.783
was, right? It all depends
on what coach

00:16:32.783 --> 00:16:35.327
you putting with
these five players.
- Right.

00:16:35.327 --> 00:16:38.205
- Because you just wasn't
going to beat us with talent.

00:16:38.205 --> 00:16:41.125
We proved that against the
Lakers, Chicago and Boston.
- Right, right.

00:16:41.125 --> 00:16:43.002
- You had to come with
a little bit more

00:16:43.002 --> 00:16:46.005
than just the best
players because we're

00:16:46.005 --> 00:16:49.133
going to make you think
for two-and-a-half hours

00:16:49.133 --> 00:16:50.634
and for 48 minutes.

00:16:50.634 --> 00:16:53.303
And if there's a weak
link on your team,

00:16:53.303 --> 00:16:54.847
oh, we're going to exploit that.

00:16:54.847 --> 00:16:59.059
- That type of mentality
is the most ferocious,

00:16:59.059 --> 00:17:01.937
mentally grinding mentality.
- Yeah.

00:17:01.937 --> 00:17:07.151
- And, like, you've proven that
that works only for y'all. Like,
- Yeah.

00:17:07.151 --> 00:17:09.361
- you can't copy that style.

00:17:09.361 --> 00:17:13.115
If you're talking the
top 25 best players ever,
- Yeah.

00:17:13.115 --> 00:17:14.658
- you beat

00:17:14.658 --> 00:17:15.325
- All of them.

00:17:15.325 --> 00:17:17.703
- three of the top 10
and their teams.

00:17:17.703 --> 00:17:19.371
All of them.
- All of them.

00:17:19.371 --> 00:17:23.792
All them people that they say
are the best to ever do it,
- Yeah.

00:17:23.792 --> 00:17:25.878
- my teams beat all of them.

00:17:25.878 --> 00:17:27.629
You know, we probably was the
best defensive team

00:17:27.629 --> 00:17:29.173
to ever play in the NBA.

00:17:29.173 --> 00:17:32.342
Now they've showed you
some highlights of us.

00:17:32.342 --> 00:17:34.845
They showed a few clips
of us fouling people hard,

00:17:34.845 --> 00:17:39.016
and then they, you know, wrote a
few paragraphs around it.

00:17:39.016 --> 00:17:41.185
And they said, OK,
this is the Pistons.

00:17:41.185 --> 00:17:43.937
But when you go back and
you watch the Pistons play

00:17:43.937 --> 00:17:46.732
and you talk to all the
coaches that you've played for,

00:17:46.732 --> 00:17:48.859
all of them are trying
- Yeah.
- to emulate

00:17:48.859 --> 00:17:50.986
the way we play defense.

00:17:50.986 --> 00:17:53.363
- They tell you from
one perspective,

00:17:53.363 --> 00:17:55.532
but they don't show
you the whole game.

00:17:55.532 --> 00:17:59.536
- Still the highest-scoring game
ever in the NBA is held by who?

00:17:59.536 --> 00:18:03.415
The Detroit Pistons
at 186 points.
- Ooh.

00:18:03.415 --> 00:18:05.459
- That's what we scored
against the Denver Nuggets.

00:18:05.459 --> 00:18:05.834
- Who were you playing?!

00:18:05.834 --> 00:18:07.127
Grandmas and grandpas?!

00:18:07.127 --> 00:18:09.379
- We was playing against
the Denver Nuggets.

00:18:09.379 --> 00:18:10.839
186-184.

00:18:10.839 --> 00:18:11.673
- You know what? It's the altitude.

00:18:11.673 --> 00:18:12.508
It's the altitude.

00:18:12.508 --> 00:18:14.593
- The highest-scoring
game ever, right?

00:18:14.593 --> 00:18:17.346
How many games have
you actually seen

00:18:17.346 --> 00:18:19.973
of the Detroit Pistons
in your lifetime?

00:18:19.973 --> 00:18:21.517
Think about that.

00:18:21.517 --> 00:18:23.352
- To be honest, off
top, I've only seen

00:18:23.352 --> 00:18:25.854
clips of Dennis Rodman
doing his thing.

00:18:25.854 --> 00:18:27.231
- Yeah, they show you a few clips—

00:18:27.231 --> 00:18:29.066
- I've seen clips of you when
you hurt your ankle.

00:18:29.066 --> 00:18:35.364
Right, but I've never—they don't really
show you guys as a whole,

00:18:35.364 --> 00:18:37.616
even though you're champions.

00:18:37.616 --> 00:18:41.245
- Those who haven't
become champions

00:18:41.245 --> 00:18:47.334
can't understand that sacrifice,
don't understand that journey.

00:18:47.334 --> 00:18:50.963
And therefore,
they lower the bar

00:18:50.963 --> 00:18:55.509
and say that, "Oh, you were just lucky
to be on the championship team."

00:18:55.509 --> 00:18:58.262
- I think we have the
greatest championship

00:18:58.262 --> 00:18:59.513
in the history of basketball.

00:18:59.513 --> 00:19:01.181
- 100 percent!
- Because we're the only—

00:19:01.181 --> 00:19:01.682
- 100 percent.

00:19:01.682 --> 00:19:03.016
- Yes. That's how I feel.

00:19:03.016 --> 00:19:08.480
- Down 3-1 in the NBA Finals
to, historically, they

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.065
were saying one of
the greatest teams

00:19:10.065 --> 00:19:15.529
to ever play and come
back and win the series.

00:19:15.529 --> 00:19:21.827
That is the greatest feat
in NBA championship play

00:19:21.827 --> 00:19:24.496
that I've ever
witnessed in my life.

00:19:24.496 --> 00:19:26.498
- The basketball
game is 40 minutes,

00:19:26.498 --> 00:19:29.251
but being a basketball
player is 24/7.

00:19:29.251 --> 00:19:29.960
- 24/7.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:33.088
- I talk to my wife four minutes.
- Yes.

00:19:33.088 --> 00:19:33.839
- I say, "Hey, baby.

00:19:33.839 --> 00:19:35.340
Hey, I love you, OK?

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:36.508
I gotta go back to my team."
- Yeah.

00:19:36.508 --> 00:19:38.385
- We're stretching together.

00:19:38.385 --> 00:19:39.803
We're eating together.

00:19:39.803 --> 00:19:41.471
We're recovering together.

00:19:41.471 --> 00:19:43.640
Even if somebody was
just getting a massage,

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:46.685
you'd have three, four
people in the room watching

00:19:46.685 --> 00:19:48.645
film, talking, sitting.
- Yeah. Yep, yep.

00:19:48.645 --> 00:19:51.523
- We were so close, right?
- Yes.

00:19:51.523 --> 00:19:54.526
- And even to this day, we're
on the same text chain.

00:19:54.526 --> 00:19:55.861
We still communicate.

00:19:55.861 --> 00:19:56.528
- Same here.

00:19:56.528 --> 00:20:00.240
- And I'm curious—to you, are
you still in communication?

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:01.909
Obviously with
Laimbeer you are.

00:20:01.909 --> 00:20:03.785
But how many of your
teammates do you

00:20:03.785 --> 00:20:06.413
talk to still after
all these years?

00:20:06.413 --> 00:20:10.667
- We have a group text
where we text every night.

00:20:10.667 --> 00:20:14.087
And in the text,
we'll say, "I love you."

00:20:14.087 --> 00:20:15.839
We'll say, you know,
"F you."

00:20:15.839 --> 00:20:16.340
- Yeah.

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:16.840
Right.

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:18.300
- Wishing each other happy birthdays.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:23.513
What you just talked about, again,
in terms of that sacrifice,

00:20:23.513 --> 00:20:25.933
that discipline.

00:20:25.933 --> 00:20:30.562
How many players are
willing to make that?

00:20:30.562 --> 00:20:35.525
- Bron plays for lineage.

00:20:35.525 --> 00:20:39.196
Bron plays for Mount
Rushmore of NBA.

00:20:39.196 --> 00:20:43.075
This man plays every game
to try to be the greatest

00:20:43.075 --> 00:20:45.285
player of all time.

00:20:45.285 --> 00:20:48.455
So when you look at his
schedule and how he eats,

00:20:48.455 --> 00:20:52.084
how he approaches a game, what
he knows, what he doesn't know,

00:20:52.084 --> 00:20:55.045
how he talks is on
a level that when

00:20:55.045 --> 00:20:59.508
you humble yourself and
say, "OK, you are different"—
- Yeah.

00:20:59.508 --> 00:21:01.593
- Then you can accept
who he is and why

00:21:01.593 --> 00:21:02.803
he acts the way he does.

00:21:02.803 --> 00:21:05.430
- I'm gonna give
you two stories and—

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:09.935
to punctuate what you
just said about LeBron.

00:21:09.935 --> 00:21:12.396
But I said to him,
I said, "You know, you have

00:21:12.396 --> 00:21:16.525
to accept the challenge of
trying to be the greatest

00:21:16.525 --> 00:21:19.987
player to ever play.
Because not many of us,

00:21:19.987 --> 00:21:24.992
including myself, has ever
had the opportunity to play

00:21:24.992 --> 00:21:28.078
for being the greatest
player to ever play.

00:21:28.078 --> 00:21:29.663
Fast-forward, right?

00:21:29.663 --> 00:21:31.206
He asked me, "OK, where do I—"

